        ReleaseNote(version: "0.6.0", name: "Overhaul",
                    when: "Sat Oct 17, 2026, 7:37 PM", build: "stamped at build",
                    install: "installed Sat Oct 17 7:58 PM over Wi-Fi (first attempt; phone reports 0.6.0)", changes: [
                        "A massive overhaul of everything (Mo, Oct 17): every algorithm family walked through with Mo and rebuilt on published methods; anything made up or of no value removed. Every personal value now starts at the published population value and moves toward yours as data grows; screens say \"population\" or \"yours (N days)\". Every method has a base tier (strap data as it comes) and an enhanced tier (raw PPG, 100 Hz motion, ECG when on), labelled.",
                        "Apple Health fixed (T10015): the upload queue had grown to 288 MB in one value and never drained since Oct 15; it now lives in its own table, the stuck batches are moved over on first launch (none lost), uploads go before new reads, and the queue is capped. This was also behind the big WAL and the 1.2 GB memory use. The database file is compacted while charging.",
                        "Sleep: new phone stager (ALG-151, trained on sleep-lab data, strap flag as the gate) replaces the old hybrid; the PC's fused stager is the primary for stages, wav2sleep and SleepECG are comparisons. Sleep need and debt come from your own strap nights (free-night average, 14-night debt), \"learning\" until enough nights. Removed: sleep cycles, restless minutes, rough night, grogginess, cardiopulmonary coupling.",
                        "Recovery: Training Readiness 1-100 (HRV status vs your baseline at its core, plus sleep, load balance and recent stress) is the headline; \"n of 5\" removed. Warning signs vs your normal (resting HR, temperature both ways, breathing, alcohol pattern, irregular rhythm, apnea screen) — never a diagnosis. Loop and Aurora fixed; noop shown as \"WHOOP-style recovery (estimate)\". HR recovery 1 and 2 min and a time constant per workout type with your own baseline. Drivers with real statistics (95% range, minimum nights).",
                        "HRV, stress, breathing: night HR dip, morning ECG check feeding HRV and readiness, signal quality, body arousal replaces stress, predicted alertness with a reaction-test self-check, breathing rate labelled estimate, SpO2 red/IR as research only until the LED identity is confirmed.",
                        "Load and body: strain headline is TRIMP in its own units, with the 0-21 as \"WHOOP-style strain (estimate)\" (fitted to your WHOOP history, held-out error 1.3 points), HR-flex calories, resting from your InBody scan (\"from scan\"), VO2max (Nes + Uth, Apple beside it), fitness age, max HR measured from your workouts; body-age \"vitality\" removed; load this week vs your 4-week average, session effort after workouts, soreness and water logs, hydration vs the EFSA reference; steps merged per minute from iPhone and strap; wear place per workout; Function Health and InBody labs.",
                        "Trends: the script proxies are relative trends against your own baseline, each saying what it stands in for and how well it is backed; new trends for breathing, apnea screen, AF burden, standing heart-rate rise, perfusion and an illness early-warning.",
                        "Workouts: when a workout is likely, raw PPG and 100 Hz motion switch on and the phone asks \"Workout? Yes / No\"; your answers teach it. Short raw-PPG bursts at chosen moments (deep sleep, morning, still times).",
                        "Strap link: every wake reconnects; switches show why they turned off; \"not connected\" says why; a notice when the app was away.",
                        "Screens: Settings and Preferences split earlier; now Today cards load lazily, quick logs have Undo, values name the source in your four terms (strap data / ours / WHOOP's / Apple's), grades shown, Log day lists, keyboard Next/Done.",
                        "Phone storage: copies the PC has confirmed go after 7 days (was 30).",
                        "Not yet checked on the phone: all of the above.",
                    ]),
        ReleaseNote(version: "0.5.9", name: "Overhaul",
                    when: "Sat Oct 17, 2026, 2:28 PM", build: "stamped at build",
                    install: "installed Sat Oct 17 by 3:42 PM over Wi-Fi (confirmed by asking the phone; the install script's stall watchdog fired during the silent final step)", changes: [
                        "Day re-send loop fixed at the root (T10002): the strap clock-set was dated at the Oct 11 pairing, so every reconnect marked Oct 11 changed and re-sent it. Clock-sets now carry the time they were written, never mark a day changed, the old ones are moved to their real day once, and the clock is only set when it is 2 s or more off.",
                        "iOS cutting the app off (T10008, T9995): when iOS ends a background run the app now stops at once; with raw streams on there is no background compute at all, the PC is asked to run the night instead, and the phone only falls back to last night itself.",
                        "Memory (T10009): night optical data is read in 30-minute slices and never in the background; a memory warning drops caches and pauses raw uploads for a minute; the memory size goes in the status log.",
                        "The database file stops growing without limit (WAL capped at 32 MB, folded in when the app goes to the background; a one-time shrink runs only while charging).",
                        "Wake chain (T10007): also starts after a history download and any background refresh, not only in the hour the strap says \"awake\".",
                        "Apple Health (T10015): last night's data is read first; the all-history backfill no longer starves it.",
                        "Choppy and slow (T23, T9904, T10001): strap decoding moved off the main thread with the screen updated about once a second; taps no longer rebuild every screen; hidden screens are built only when opened; switches move at once instead of snapping back; faster open (database opens off the main thread, the saved front page shows first; a Bluetooth relaunch starts only the strap link, plus a light check every 5 minutes for smart wake, alarms and the wake chain).",
                        "Live PPG (T10005): drawn as one line per plot with peaks kept and a steady scale, the same signal no longer drawn three times, waves in one collapsed \"Waves\" section under the action buttons.",
                        "100 Hz motion (T10006): the strap's full answer is logged; on a refusal the app stops, waits and retries once; it never starts during a history download.",
                        "History download (T10013): the % can now reach the end; near the end it says \"catching up to now\", then \"caught up to 9:41 AM\".",
                        "Sleep page (T10011): \"Not processed yet\" until the strap flags for the night are in, then the strap-flag time asleep, then phone and PC stages, each labelled, with a phone-vs-PC stage comparison. The ring, hub and Today no longer show a rising partial total or yesterday's night before the morning compute.",
                        "Sundial (T10021, T10023): the scrub starts as soon as you drag (no hold), the page stops scrolling under it, drag outward to step ring to ring, tap to magnify, the time scrub shows heart rate, caffeine and flags with their source. Strain and stress have no time series yet and say so.",
                        "Settings and Preferences (T10022): the M circle opens Preferences (you and how the app looks); the gear opens Settings (strap, PC, data, tests, about). iOS references say \"iOS Settings › MG\".",
                        "Screen fixes (T10021): End workout asks first; dead accordion arrows fixed; HRV-by-method shows one empty card instead of 80 repeats; negative longitude can be typed; Log keeps half-typed text and has a keyboard Done key; Command test has Send buttons and search; Tonight copy matches the alarm setting.",
                        "Phone storage: the phone keeps one raw copy; copies the PC has confirmed go after 30 days; per-hour counters replace full-table scans. PC results now come in batches instead of one request per file.",
                        "PC Lab: the job queue survives restarts and the phone's request runs first (T10016, T10018); models run in a separate low-priority process so a stuck model cannot stall the server; raw optical becomes a PPG stream for wav2sleep (T10014); one time zone from the phone; an hourly gap ledger; the watcher logs why it restarts the server.",
                        "Not yet checked on the phone: all of the above. First launch of this build reads the raw table once to build the hourly counters (may take a while on the 1.3 GB database).",
                    ]),
